# Program ledger — append-only, one JSON object per line.
# Schema: docs/reference/PROGRAM_MANAGEMENT_SPEC.md section 4 (issue #31).
# Written by program/ledger.py, which enforces the section 4 rules on append.
#
# Keys (exactly these, every record):
#   id         string        sequential "P-001", "P-002", ... stable, orderable, unique
#   ts         string        UTC ISO8601, when the record was written
#   issue      integer       the GitHub issue this records the outcome of
#   run_id     string        the agent run that produced it (<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>-<4 alnum>)
#   kind       string        spec section 3.1 value, without the "kind:" prefix
#   outcome    string|null   spec section 3.2 value; null while in flight
#   rung       integer|null  spec section 3.3 value if the outcome is a claim; else null
#   results    list[string]  repo-relative paths to result artifacts this outcome rests on
#   decisions  list[string]  "DEC-0NN" ids this outcome produced or rests on
#   findings   list[integer] 1-based line numbers in findings.jsonl, if any
#   blocks     list[integer] issue numbers this outcome unblocks or redirects
#   emergent   list[integer] issue numbers this work created (spec section 6 path)
#   note       string        short prose; not a place for conclusions
#
# Deliberately absent: issue titles, statuses, assignees, priority — all already
# in GitHub, and copying them creates the drift the coverage-map decision warns
# against (spec section 4).
#
# outcome is the INTERPRETATION of a result; verdict in findings.jsonl is its
# LABEL. A `flagged` verdict can be either instrument-validated (the #5 control)
# or phenomenon-present (a real finding) — they are not the same field
# (spec section 10 Q3).
